          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How often does guidance describe checking thyroid function once treatment is stable?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance does not say how often to check thyroid function once treatment is stable.\n\nThe closest match is the Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance, titled \"Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change\" (guideline-hypothyroidism-testing-and-monitoring). It says the thyroid-stimulating hormone test is repeated six to eight weeks after starting thyroid hormone replacement or after any dose change, because it takes that long to settle at the new level. That covers the period after a change, not the long-term interval.\n\nOther documents in the results give stable-phase intervals for other conditions, such as blood pressure (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment) and cholesterol (guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation), but not for thyroid testing. I can't fill that gap from general knowledge. A clinician who manages the treatment can tell you what schedule suits your situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
